  "narrative": "During the 1990s, Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture continued to thrive, reflecting a deep connection to their heritage and identity. The natural hair movement, which had originated in the 1960s, saw a resurgence in popularity during this decade, with many women embracing their afro-textured hair in all its glory. Styles such as Afros, cornrows, and box braids became increasingly popular, not only for their beauty but also for their cultural significance. These hairstyles were often worn as protective styles, designed to minimize manipulation and exposure of the hair to environmental elements. The Jheri curl, a chemical treatment that gave the wearer a glossy, loosely curled look, was also popular during this time. While documentation from this era is limited in the provided source material, it is clear that Black and African women's hairstyles continued to be an integral part of their identity and cultural expression.",
